{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact head 95fbdefd50a9a033e5227141f89d350c6356d504, resting on all 19 artifacts with docs/protocol.md 1b41810a first.\n\nThe required Lane C shapes are correctly adopted: path/owners pairs, complete ReplayContext and Decision.input binding, versioned aggregate limits/accounting, pinned immutable policy retention, CheckCarryFacts, explicit retirement/sole-admin reopening reasons and lane purpose. Local shadow types are removed, and the ten documented gaps are resolved or explicitly kept open. Preserve these changes.\n\nP1: Define the prospective reservation input and the synchronous comparison (docs/protocol.md R-LAND-4, R-LAND-7 and R-POL-6; contract policy.ts RuleInput.stage; landing.ts ready.landInput). The new R-LAND-7 wording identifies landInput as the digest of the entire bare land RuleInput. That input contains policy-visible stage: land | reservation, but the contract never specifies which stage preparation evaluates at ready. A valid rule block: 'stage = \"reservation\"' passes at stage land and refuses at stage reservation. With all other state unchanged, those bare input digests also differ. If an implementation changes stage when rebuilding at reservation, every unchanged preparation can mismatch and retry; if it retains stage land to make the digest match, it skips reservation-specific policy. Different lanes should not have to infer this authorization boundary.\n\nSpecify that land admission evaluates stage land, while preparation evaluates the prospective reservation input with stage reservation before it becomes ready; reservation rebuilds that same stage from current state. Retain the successful evaluation's canonical bare input bytes and their digest, separate from its complete replay context. Explain that the no-await SQLite reservation transaction compares synchronously rebuilt canonical bytes against those retained bytes (with all current authority/evidence checks); WebCrypto hashing and evaluation